An IRC channel can be designed as a private forum with 
/mode #channel +i (perhaps +ptsi).
This would clearly declare it as a private forum, since it requires an
explicit invitation from the channel op (i.e. the channel creator(s))
to join. If some other joins without being invited, he exploits a
software bug or abuses IRC operator status and therefore can be judged
as disrupting. The channel creators can also ban and boot off any
person from that channel, thus qualifying the channel for status as
private place even more.

Since *every* IRC channel can be made private with this commands upon
cration, I think that other IRC channels (who haven't chosen to run
private) are public places.

(Besides this obvious technical solution, I am amazed how people try to
solve every potential conflict with legal means. Perhaps I'm just on
the wrong side of the Atlantic, but I really don't understand these
heated debates over the meaning of single words in some laws and
regulations - I have really good experience with solving most problems
on an informal level...)

