	I agree.  The freedom of speech of a person is ONLY violated if he/she
is prevented from expressing his/her opinion BY THE GOVERNMENT.  Every private institution has the right to give access to its own property (e.g. computer account) to any individual.  On the other hand, it also has the right to withdraw those access any time if it so wishes.

	Therefore, one's freedom of speech is NOT violated even if his/her account is disabled because of the unfavourable opinion he/she expresses through the account. (unfavourable as determined by the private institution, who actually OWNS the account).

	On the other hand, the University of Washington has every right to continue to give anyone (even 'Jammer') access to the account if it so desires.  Again, the computer accounts (i.e. the computing facilities) are the properties of the University and it has the right (property right) to give access to them to whoever it chooses to.

	So I think it is inappropriate for the Director of the University Computing Services to cite "freedom of speech" as the reason of not taking any action towards 'Jammer', unless it is the University's own policy to let anyone to use it facilities to express any opinion he/she wants.  If that happens to be the case, what we should do is to condemn the person who express those opinions, as what many people here has done so far.




