In article <C4tn8G.GGD@andy.bgsu.edu> hreutte@andy.bgsu.edu (Jean-Luc for you counselor just Jean-Luc) writes:
> julf@penet.fi (Ze Julf) writes:
> >This faked posting was brought to my attention. As you can see from the
> >headers, this message did *not* orginate from anon.penet.fi, but is a (rather
> >clumsy) forgery posted from either andy.bgsu.edu or m64-135.bgsu.edu. It just
> >illustrates how easy it is just as easy to abuse the network without any
> >anonymous servers.

> >> Newsgroups: alt.sex
> >> Path: ...!magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu!bgsuvax!m64-135.bgsu.edu!user
> >> From: AN18656@ANON.PENET.FI. (Mo-dog )
> >>[Stupid trash, the sort you want to hide your identity for]

> Julf is right!
> This is a fake anon message, posted from Bowling Green State University,
> Bowling Green, Ohio, USA.
> Unfortunately there are many MacIntoshs in the undergraduate dormitories that
> are connected directly to andy.bgsu.edu, the BGSU usenet connection and
> require NO user authorization or user ID. Among many of these undergraduate
> residents and their friends it is common 'cool thing to do', to post all kinds
> of offending articles on the net. Usually these articles are about football and
> college basketball ("Your team sucks, mine is the greatest") but recently there
> have been more and more posts like the one above.

> Why they allow access to the net without an user ID remains a mystery to me!

It's simple: On the Net level, there is no such thing as a User ID.
Since a Macintosh, unlike Unix or VMS, doesn't provide for User IDs by
itself, you can enter just anything into the appropriate field on all
Mac newsreaders - i.e. every user can define hir own From: lines.

The only means to stop this would be to cut off IP access from those
machines, but that is most probably disruptive to normal operation.

> What to do about it?
> I don't know. I think it is best to ignore these posts, even if they are
> obviously written by rather racistic students like the one above...
> By the way: these posts from the dormitories can be easily identified by the
> Mxx-xxx MacIntosh terminal number.

Of course you can, but how does the world know how to?
(Here we have a similar numbering scheme for the office Macs:
NBI-UG-MAC12 is Mac #12 in the basement (UG) of the new CS building
(NBI) and while such numbers at least look suspicious, it's generally
not possible to tell single-user boxes from multi-user boxes [wrt. OS]
just by their IP numbers or DNS names...)

Julf has shown how to identify such postings as forgeries - by
analyzing the Path header (but this requires knowledge about RFC 1036
and those things, a skill not to be expected from the occasional
simple user who probably feels most annoyed by such postings.)

My question is now: as I am in great favor of equipping dorms with
network connections and providing public access terminals (or Macs,
PCs or other things which don't have user ID) I'd like to know how
this obvious problem - abuse by news forgeries, or other potential
abuses based on the absence of user IDs - is handled in other
places. 

A potential solution would be to firewall the "dangerous" network
sections, i.e. require every Internet access to run via telnet over a
trusted (with user IDs) host - but this could, depending on the
precise situation, be infeasible or undesirable. Furthermore, this can
probably be circumvented with not much more effort than required for
the original forgery (with most NNTP servers, providing an arbitrary
From: line is trivial, and to get aroung the automatic Sender:
insertion isn't too hard...)

Can anyone suggest better solutions?

Olaf
